:answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425641} :answer-field/id #uuid "9935b968-041b-5391-998c-53108d7a9f15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c7a0680-8ba5-57b0-b5fa-884bcd638943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425596} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5a544a-4601-5833-b1db-6a435d610e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425603} {:answer/id #uuid "aa351d07-d824-58b7-91ec-790920890986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425614} {:answer/id #uuid "fac3f35e-247f-5c4f-a920-7b829128be6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa351d07-d824-58b7-91ec-790920890986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425614} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5970db6-cf36-573d-8824-86ad5ee154aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c9cd435-4b0a-516c-bede-18c713c259dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343389" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-5{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-15+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-15t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{540(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #